<h1>combination</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Choose m of n elements, where m &le; n.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="permutation.html"><em>permutation</em></a>, <a href="subset.html"><em>subset</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A combination is a subset with exactly m elements. </p> 
<p> There are <a href="factorial.html"><em>n!/(n-m)!m!</em></a> combinations of n (distinguishable) elements taken m at a time.  Why?  To begin, there are n! <a href="permutation.html"><em>permutations</em></a>.  Permutations that only differ in the order of the n-m elements not taken are the same combination, so divide by (n-m)!.  The order of the m elements taken doesn't matter either, so divide by m!, too.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

